217442531"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-87ba764f-e6be-49ac-af3c-235def6f972b","au-c7024848-3bf8-4c4f-9bb4-d0689830c925","sdu-82fa5766-cd5c-45ef-b638-689440f77fd9"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Endothelial and neuronal engagement by AAV-BR1 gene therapy alleviates neurological symptoms and lipid deposition in a mouse model of Niemann-Pick type C2","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-100.00-792938","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Biochar is receiving increasing political attention as a scalable and relevant climate change mitigation tool. With this growing focus comes a growing need for more accurate assessments of biochar climate change impact potentials. The mechanism of biochar soil carbon climate change mitigation is the time offsetting of emissions through decreased soil carbon decay. Therefore, temporally dynamic modelling is necessary to accurately assess these impacts. In this study, the 100-year evolution of soil carbon following soil application of biochar, residual straw and agricultural biogas digestate is modelled considering uncertainty related to biochar stability and environmental variability. From soil carbon modelling, dynamic climate change impacts are determined for biochar, straw and digestate per unit carbon added to soil for use in life cycle assessment. The developed methods are showcased in impact assessments of biochar at two levels of systems perspective. Results show that the developed dynamic modelling methods improve the accuracy of soil carbon climate change impact assessment. By disregarding short-term soil carbon storage, static modelling underestimates the impacts of non-pyrolyzed biomass soil carbon by a factor of two to three. Biochar is shown to provide a significant net climate benefit through increased soil carbon storage. In a large-scale implementation scenario, it is shown that biochar can contribute beneficially towards the Paris agreement 2100 temperature target, but also that national emissions accounting methods and short-term political climate targets can become a barrier for political incentives to realize the climate change benefits of biochar.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Karlsson, Magnus Bo","Kamp, Annette","Thomsen, Tobias Pape"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-830529"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biochar","Biomass","Climate change","Dynamic LCA","Pyrolysis","Soil carbon"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dynamic assessment of biochar soil carbon climate change impacts","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2025.180058","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-830529","identifier":[{"id":"105010851078","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-0650a1ee-4daf-4b15-8c9f-d9ba48497021"],"keyword":["Biochar","Biomass","Climate change","Dynamic LCA","Pyrolysis","Soil carbon"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-9974B6558221634391583E42063F105B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2025.180058","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/111790362/1-s2.0-S0048969725016985-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology","Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology, TRANSITIONS – Sustainability Transitions and Environmental Planning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"M. B.","key":"karlssonpau","last":"Karlsson","locid":"d999fa2c-01a5-481d-a408-5cb578b8d3ee","name":"Karlsson, Magnus Bo","name_org":"Karlsson, M. B.","orcid":"0009-0003-2691-9365","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"A.","key":"kamppau","last":"Kamp","locid":"1725aee0-5cec-463c-9b01-3fcfb1142b81","name":"Kamp, Annette","name_org":"Kamp, A.","orcid":"0000-0001-8867-8796","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"T. P.","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"e0f8cb5c-1327-4f91-99a5-2da7ebda73e8","name":"Thomsen, Tobias Pape","name_org":"Thomsen, T. P.","orcid":"0000-0003-3597-7199","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2025.180058","issn":["00489697","18791026"],"issn_org":["00489697"],"journal":"Science of the Total Environment","paperid":"180058","status":"p","title":"Science of the Total Environment","volume":"995"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010851078"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-0650a1ee-4daf-4b15-8c9f-d9ba48497021"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Dynamic assessment of biochar soil carbon climate change impacts","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-830529","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The lack of essential information when reporting animal studies causing lower reproducibility has been stressed for decades. The ARRIVE (Animal Research: Reporting of In Vivo Experiments) guidelines were first published in 2010, to improve reporting of animal research, making in vivo studies more transparent thereby improving the scientific quality. Regardless of an endorsement from the scientific community, there is still a continuous need to improve animal research reporting, which unfortunately also is the case in the field of Niemann-Pick type C disease (NPC). NPC is a lipid storage disorder, caused by mutations in either the Npc1 or Npc2 gene. Despite years of research, no cure for this fatal disease exists. In 2020, an updated version of the ARRIVE guidelines (ARRIVE 2.0), was published, describing the ten most essential elements to be included when reporting pre-clinical studies. Here we systematically reviewed the compliance with the ARRIVE guidelines using the \"ARRIVE Essential 10\" checklist in a series of pre-clinical studies investigating gene therapy as a treatment strategy for NPC. None of the reviewed papers fulfilled the ARRIVE 2.0 guidelines. Information regarding sample size, randomization, blinding, and statistical methodology was lacking. Hopefully, the newly updated ARRIVE guidelines will aid researchers in planning and publishing in vivo experiments in the future. More awareness of the importance of including these essential items is needed, both from editors, reviewers and researchers, for complete endorsement of the ARRIVE guidelines in the scientific community.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","Burkhart, Annette","Moos, Torben","Thomsen, Louiza Bohn"],"cuid":["S02-095.45-803681"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Rasmussen, Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","Thomsen, Louiza Bohn"],"keyword":["ARRIVE 2 0","ARRIVE guidelines","Animals","Gene therapy","Genetic Therapy methods","Humans","NPC","Niemann Pick Disease Type C therapy","Niemann Pick type C disease"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reporting preclinical gene therapy studies in the field of niemann pick type c disease according to the arrive guidelines","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13023-024-03479-1","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was kindly funded by Lundbeck Foundation\\u2014Research Initiative on Blood\\u2013Brain Barriers and Drug Delivery (Grant no. 2013-14113), H\\u00F8rslev-Fonden, L\\u00E6ge Sophus Carl Emil Friis og Hustru Olga Doris Friis Legat."],"id":"S02-095.45-803681","identifier":[{"id":"PMC12054331","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"105004301620","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40329398","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-4ca5a0ee-68ee-41f7-805a-c7203c57ba29","sdu-294a7b46-d395-44b9-82fa-02711315eb43"],"keyword":["ARRIVE 2.0","ARRIVE guidelines","Animals","Gene therapy","Genetic Therapy/methods","Humans","NPC","Niemann-Pick Disease, Type C/therapy","Niemann-Pick type C disease"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0AFEA859CF758EC2B36BAF5475BE3143"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/289628335/s13023-024-03479-1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/779084524/s13023-024-03479-1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13023-024-03479-1","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology, Neurobiology Research and Drug Delivery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"608faa0d-42e3-46c7-bbeb-95118471e801","name":"Rasmussen, Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","orcid":"0000-0001-6535-3729","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Annette","key":"burkhartpau","last":"Burkhart","locid":"7b6bd909-a011-463c-9e09-3623fee043b3","name":"Burkhart, Annette","orcid":"0000-0003-2086-4693","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Torben","key":"moospau","last":"Moos","locid":"e51cbd29-97d2-42d8-bbca-11f026378a2f","name":"Moos, Torben","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louiza Bohn","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"c4698d51-553a-4391-90b0-5379982b935f","name":"Thomsen, Louiza Bohn","orcid":"0000-0002-3144-7150","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40329398"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s13023-024-03479-1","issn":["17501172"],"issue":"1","journal":"Orphanet Journal of Rare Diseases","paperid":"214","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"Orphanet Journal of Rare Diseases","volume":"20"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105004301620"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-4ca5a0ee-68ee-41f7-805a-c7203c57ba29","sdu-294a7b46-d395-44b9-82fa-02711315eb43"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Reporting preclinical gene therapy studies in the field of Niemann-Pick type C disease according to the ARRIVE guidelines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.45-803681","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Following publication of the original article [1], the authors reported that at the end of the section “Outcome measures,” a paragraph is absent. Furthermore, Table 7 is missing. (Table presented.) Outcome measures Outcome measures References Motor coordination Rotarod [12, 13] Gait analysis* [11, 12, 14–16] Balance beam [11, 14] Locomotor activity Open field [13–15] Tremor Automated tremor sensor [16] Purkinje cell degeneration Calbindin staining [10, 12–16] Nissl staining [15] H&E [13, 22] Cholesterol accumulation Filipin staining [10, 11, 14, 15] Lipid accumulation GM2 staining [11] Glycogen accumulation PAS staining [14] Neuroinflammation Astrogliosis (GFAP) [11, 14–16, 22] Microglia (CD68, Iba-1) [11, 14–16] H&E [11, 14, 22] Inflammation CD68 [10, 11] Cholesterol accumulation Filipin [10, 11, 12, 15] H&E [14, 22] Cholesterol accumulation Filipin [15, 22] *Gait analysis included automated gait analysis using the Noldus CatWalk [15, 16] visual inspection of footprints [14], clinical description [12], and gait scoring scheme [11] The paragraph and Table 7 have now been added and are the following: “The involvement of the cerebellum with progressive Purkinje cell loss in NPC disease becomes evident when the patient develops symptoms such as tremors and ataxia, which are also evident in Npc-/- mouse models [1, 33–36]. Furthermore, these severe symptoms affect the ability to accomplish normal behavioral tasks involving motor activity [14, 37]. Assessment of motor coordination is therefore often included when evaluating gene therapy in Npc-/- studies, and six out of eight studies included in this review, have also assessed motor function using, e.g., the balance beam, open field activity, rotarod, and/or gait analysis (Table 7). Lastly, a broad spectrum of post-mortem parameters was included in the outcome measures, with the main focus on neuropathology. Purkinje cell degeneration is the most commonly evaluated parameter using the Purkinje cell marker Calbindin [38] and seven out of eight papers have analyzed Purkinje cell degeneration in the Npc-/- mouse model (Table 7). As mentioned earlier, Purkinje cell loss is a hallmark of the NPC disease, and the development of symptoms is related to the extent of Purkinje cell degeneration. Thus improvement in lifespan is usually accompanied by the preservation of these vulnerable cerebellar neurons [10, 39]. The information found in Table 7 can provide an overview of different methods to be included in future studies on NPC disease. It has been proposed that registering a protocol in advance, as seen with clinical trials, can avoid data dredging also called p-hacking. This means selective reporting of outcome measures that were statistically significant or collecting more data afterward in a search for significant results [20, 40–42]. However, this calls for a general change in the field of research where publication bias is a major challenge. There is evidence that studies with positive or statistically significant (p < 0.05) results are more likely to get published, and that neutral or negative findings take longer to publish, are published in low impact, or are not published at all [41, 43]. This is indeed worrisome as publication bias can lead to overestimation of, e.g., treatment effects [41], which undoubtedly further challenges the translation of results from animal studies to humans.” The original article [1] has been updated.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","Burkhart, Annette","Moos, Torben","Thomsen, Louiza Bohn"],"cuid":["S02-091.30-822266"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Rasmussen, Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","Thomsen, Louiza Bohn"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"correction to reporting preclinical gene therapy studies in the field of niemann pick type c disease according to the arrive guidelines orphanet journal of rare diseases 000000002025 000000000020 000000000001 000000000214 000000000010 000000001186 s000000013023 000000000024 000000003479 000000000001","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"djc","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13023-025-03826-w","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-091.30-822266","identifier":[{"id":"105007734875","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40495191","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC12153099","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["aau-2bf4ee84-688c-4939-97f0-222f1f348ed7","sdu-24b73751-a341-4c81-8296-9482331c9d33"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4688BC0BBE93E12A0C7C8A9936532BDC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13023-025-03826-w","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Biomedical Laboratory"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"f88d5158-1bb2-4d13-96a8-1bde6382593b","name":"Rasmussen, Charlotte Laurfelt Munch","orcid":"0