{"abstract":"BACKGROUND/OBJECTIVES: Iron (Fe) is a co-factor for enzymes of the developing brain necessitating sufficient supply. We investigated the effects of administering ferric derisomaltose/Fe isomaltoside (FDI) subcutaneously to Fe-deficient (ID) pregnant rats on cerebral and hepatic concentrations of essential metals and the expression of iron-relevant genes. METHODS: Pregnant rats subjected to ID were injected with FDI on the day of mating (E0), 14 days into pregnancy (E14), or the day of birth (postnatal (P0)). The efficacy was evaluated by determination of cerebral and hepatic Fe, copper (Cu), and zinc (Zn) and gene expression of ferroportin, hepcidin, and ferritin H + L in pups on P0 and as adults on P70. RESULTS: Females fed an ID diet (5.2 mg/kg Fe) had offspring with significantly lower cerebral and hepatic Fe compared to female controls fed a standard diet (158 mg/kg Fe). Cerebral Cu increased irrespective of supplying a standard diet or administering FDI combined with the standard diet. Hepatic hepcidin mRNA was significantly lower following ID. Cerebral hepcidin mRNA was hardly detectable irrespective of iron status. CONCLUSIONS: In conclusion, administering FDI subcutaneously to ID pregnant rats on E0 normalizes fetal cerebral and hepatic Fe. When applied at later gestational ages, supplementation with additional Fe to the offspring is needed to normalize cerebral and hepatic Fe.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Burkhart, Annette","Johnsen, Kasper Bendix","Skjørringe, Tina","Nielsen, Asbjørn Haaning","Routhe, Lisa Juul","Hertz, Sandra","Møller, Lisbeth Birk","Thomsen, Lars Lykke","Moos, Torben"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-784746"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Moos, Torben"],"keyword":["Anemia Iron Deficiency drug therapy","Animals","Brain metabolism","Cation Transport Proteins metabolism","Copper","Copper metabolism","Disaccharides","Female","Ferroportin","Fetus metabolism","Hepcidin","Iron","Iron Deficiencies","Iron deficiency","Iron metabolism","Liver metabolism","Pregnancy","Rats","Rats Wistar","Zinc","Zinc deficiency"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"normalization of fetal cerebral and hepatic iron by parental iron therapy to pregnant rats with systemic iron deficiency without anemia","src":["aau","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu16193264","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The present study has been supported by the Simon Fougner Hartmanns Family Foundation (Grant no: not applicable), the Danish Innovation Fund (Grant no: 014-2011-5), and the Lundbeck Foundation Research Initiative on Brain Barriers and Drug Delivery (Grant no. R155-2013-14113) to Torben Moos."],"id":"S02-100.00-784746","identifier":[{"id":"85206514543","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39408231","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC11479134","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["aau-046553d6-91d5-4672-81c0-c9a3c3c028a8","dtu-1cc3aea0-e142-4574-be9e-7664e6924ef4"],"keyword":["Anemia, Iron-Deficiency/drug therapy","Animals","Brain/metabolism","Cation Transport Proteins/metabolism","Copper/metabolism","Disaccharides","Female","Fetus/metabolism","Hepcidins/metabolism","Iron Deficiencies","Iron/metabolism","Liver/metabolism","Pregnancy","Rats","Rats, Wistar","Zinc/deficiency","copper","ferroportin","hepcidin","iron","iron deficiency","zinc"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-0C4DC6AB2C07DDA41837902B057DAD9D"],"oa_link":[{"ac