"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"OLAMUR Offshore low-trophic aquaculture in multi-use scenario realization","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-717254","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Multi-use of offshore wind farms with low-trophic aquaculture could provide sustainable energy, nutritious seafood, and restorative ecosystem services through nutrients and carbon capture and utilization. In a transition zone between marine and brackish seas, our model predicted that allocating 10% of projected wind farm areas to blue mussel and sugar kelp aquaculture in the North Sea - Baltic Sea transition zone could yield 18 t-fresh weight ha−1 yr−1. Total carbon captured and harvested from seaweed biomass and mussel shells would equal 40% of the carbon dioxide emissions from the Danish agricultural sector. Furthermore, global aquaculture production is projected to increase by 132% compared to current production. With technological and regulatory challenges still to be addressed, these findings demonstrate a vast potential of multi-use in offshore areas, which can generate blue biomass with fewer user conflicts, while mitigating eutrophication and climate change, thereby supporting multiple global sustainable development goals.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Maar, Marie","Holbach, Andreas Michael","Boderskov, Teis","Thomsen, Marianne","Buck, Bela H.","Kotta, Jonne","Bruhn, Annette"],"cuid":["S02-096.94-716023"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Maar, Marie"],"country":["Estonia","Germany"],"glorg":["Alfred Wegener Institute - Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research","All Global Affiliations","Bremerhaven University of Applied Sciences","Helmholtz Association of German Research Centres","University of Tartu"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multi use of offshore wind farms with low trophic aquaculture can help achieve global sustainability goals","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-023-01116-6","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank 4C Offshore, a TGS company for allowing the use and publication of the wind farm area data and the Vattenfall BA Wind GIS Team, in particular Lisa Holland, for their support in extracting data and preparing the global map shown in Fig. . Vattenfall BA Wind Bioscience team, in particular Matthieu Povidis-Delefosse is thanked for the global OWF projections and comments on earlier version of the manuscript. Tinna Christensen is acknowledged for final graphics of Fig. . Jacob Carstensen is thanked for input to an earlier version of this manuscript. This research has been funded from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 innovation programme under grant agreement No. 101094065 OLAMUR and the Danish Velux Foundation WIN@sea."],"id":"S02-096.94-716023","identifier":[{"id":"85178192952","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8273b78c-676b-4a9c-9b8d-74f9424e2d7d","ku-29e1d399-26a3-4429-a15b-3cc69f343b16"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-DDD31EF25AC72DF170FBAF1CDF782ADF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/374648498/Multi_use_of_offshore_wind_farms_with_low_trophic_aquaculture_can_help_achieve_global_sustainability_goals.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-023-01116-6","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Ecoscience, Department of Ecoscience - Applied Marine Ecology and Modelling"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Food Science, Design and Consumer Behavior"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"helmholtz association of german research centres","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Helmholtz Association of German Research Centres","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"bremerhaven university of applied sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Bremerhaven University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"alfred wegener institute helmholtz centre for polar and marine research","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Alfred Wegener Institute - Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"ee","key":"university of tartu","loc":["Estonia","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Tartu","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Marie","key":"maarpau","last":"Maar","locid":"82a27bc5-38c0-4f2b-808e-b451f47d5adc","name":"Maar, Marie","orcid":"0000-0001-8594-2993","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"holbachpau","last":"Holbach","locid":"e861bdea-068c-47a9-9dfd-30d68afdec9e","name":"Holbach, Andreas Michael","name_org":"Holbach, Andreas","orcid":"0000-0002-7665-9341","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Teis","key":"boderskovpau","last":"Boderskov","locid":"c63d7c51-d48b-4a3f-a126-3b71bd450fc6","name":"Boderskov, Teis","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Marianne","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"a829b47d-f5d2-404d-b768-ec12fbb3b661","name":"Thomsen, Marianne","orcid":"0000-0003-2453-5141","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Bela H.","key":"buckpau","last":"Buck","name":"Buck, Bela H.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonne","key":"kottapau","last":"Kotta","name":"Kotta, Jonne","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Annette","key":"bruhnpau","last":"Bruhn","locid":"15559017-3036-4e04-968e-338a24d800ce","name":"Bruhn, Annette","orcid":"0000-0002-7940-1338","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s43247-023-01116-6","issn":["26624435"],"issue":"1","journal":"Communications Earth and Environment","paperid":"447","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"Communications Earth and Environment","title_org":"Communications Earth & Environment","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85178192952"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8273b78c-676b-4a9c-9b8d-74f9424e2d7d","ku-29e1d399-26a3-4429-a15b-3cc69f343b16"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multi-use of offshore wind farms with low-trophic aquaculture can help achieve global sustainability goals","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.94-716023","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Patients newly diagnosed with inflammatory arthritis (IA) request regular consultations and support from health professionals to manage physiological, emotional, and social challenges. Evidence suggests that providing a tailored multi-component self-management program may benefit disease management. However, there is a lack of evidence of effective interventions with multiple components targeting the needs of this group. Therefore, the aim of this study was to develop a self-management intervention targeting newly diagnosed patients with IA, following the Medical Research Council (MRC) framework for developing complex interventions. Methods: The development of the complex self-management intervention covered three steps. First, the evidence base was identified through literature reviews, in which we described a preliminary nurse-led intervention. Secondly, we chose Social Cognitive Theory as the underlying theory along with Acceptance and Commitment Theory to support our communication strategy. Thirdly, the preliminary intervention was discussed and further developed in workshops to ensure that the intervention was in accordance with patients’ needs and feasible in clinical practice. Results: The developed intervention comprises a 9-month nurse-led intervention (four individual and two group sessions). A physiotherapist and an occupational therapist will attend the group sessions along with the nurse. All sessions should target IA-specific self-management with a particular focus on medical, role, and emotional management. Conclusion: Through the workshops, we involved all levels of the organization to optimize the intervention, but also to create ownership and commitment, and to identify barriers and shortcomings of the preliminary intervention. As a result, from the existing evidence, we believe that we have identified effective mechanisms to increase self-management in people newly diagnosed with IA. Further, we believe that the involvement of various stakeholders has contributed significantly to developing a relevant and feasible intervention. The intervention is a nurse-led complex self-management intervention embedded in a multidisciplinary team (named NISMA). The intervention is currently being tested in a feasibility study.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Lindgren, L. H.","Thomsen, Thordis","de Thurah, A.","Aadahl, Mette","Hetland, Merete Lund","Kristensen, S. D.","Esbensen, Bente Appel"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-021679"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Lindgren, Lars"],"keyword":["Complex interventions","Development","Inflammatory arthritis","Newly diagnosed and multi disciplinary intervention","Self management"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"newly diagnosed with inflammatory arthritis nisma development of a complex self management intervention","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-022-09007-w","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-021679","identifier":[{"id":"36750937","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC9902823","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85147594719","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-7b994988-b285-40cd-a9f9-d80ec543724b","ku-e6c023c6-7f83-4dd9-b3ff-1a6c06372c4e"],"keyword":["Complex interventions","Development","Inflammatory arthritis","Newly diagnosed and multi-disciplinary intervention","Self-management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-77CD06D71889F73A47D261E05A349E5E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi"