{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Point-of-care ultrasonography (POCUS) is increasingly used in general practice despite the lack of official educational programmes or guidelines for general practitioners (GPs). AIM: To explore how GPs have learnt to use POCUS and which barriers they have encountered in their learning process. DESIGN AND SETTING: Qualitative study conducted in office-based general practice in Denmark. METHODS: Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 13 GPs who had implemented POCUS without supporting guidelines or regulations. Analysis was carried out using systematic text condensation. The interview data for this study were collected along with data used in a previous study. RESULTS: The participating GPs described having composed their own ultrasound education following a continuous learning process. Basic POCUS competences were achieved through formalized training sessions at hospital departments or courses. The GPs further developed and expanded their scanning skills through additional courses and continuous self-study practice on patients often while consulting internet sources, textbooks or colleagues. Lack of available ultrasound courses, supervision, and clinical guidelines together with time constraints and financial aspects were mentioned as barriers to their ultrasound training. CONCLUSION: This study showed how GPs had composed their own ultrasound education individually and differently, guided by their own experiences and beliefs about good clinical practice. Formalized ultrasound training was considered a prerequisite for achieving basic ultrasound competences while continuous practice was considered paramount to develop and maintain scanning skills. There were several obstacles to overcome in the learning process including lack of supervision, guidance, and opportunity for practicing skills.Key pointsLittle is known about the educational needs of general practitioners striving to achieve ultrasound competences.General practitioners described using formalized training to achieve basic scanning competences and continuous self-study and practice to further develop their skills.Lack of time, supervision, clinical guidelines and ultrasound courses were considered barriers in the learning process together with financial aspects.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Andersen, Camilla Aakjær","Espersen, Marie","Brodersen, John","Thomsen, Janus Laust","Jensen, Martin Bach","Davidsen, Annette Sofie"],"cuid":["S02-098.43-011837"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Diagnostic methods","Education","Family medicine","General Practitioners","General practice","Humans","Medicine","Point of Care Systems","Point of Care Testing","Qualitative Research","Ultrasonography"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"learning strategies of general practitioners striving to achieve point of care ultrasound competence a qualitative study","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/02813432.2022.2036483","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-098.43-011837","identifier":[{"id":"000755505300001","type":"wos"},{"id":"35166178","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC9090417","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85125228402","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-f7cb8ea6-1e3f-4684-a52d-7161037c7fa5","ku-a6bd4757-6a08-490d-8745-8f8843f061af"],"keyword":["Diagnostic methods","General Practitioners","Humans","MEDICINE","Point-of-Care Systems","Point-of-Care Testing","education","family medicine","general practice","qualitative research","ultrasonography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-773F2326BD1C349DE323CD2942340BD2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/519456803/Aakjaer_Andersen_2022_Learning_strategies_of_general_practitioners_striving_to_achieve_point_of_care_ultrasound_competence_a_qualitative_study.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/02813432.2022.2036483","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/305001668/Learning.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine, Center for General Practice at Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, External Centres, Forskningsenheden for Almen Praksis"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Region of Zealand"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Camilla Aakjaer","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"7fe4e7b3-746c-4755-b532-839957f0125b","name":"Andersen, Camilla Vaskjær Aakjær","name_org":"Andersen, Camilla Aakjaer","orcid":"0000-0002-5933-748X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"espersenpau","last":"Espersen","locid":"0e87cf9d-d0d3-4453-abff-a021d1e70d15","name":"Espersen, Marie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"John","key":"brodersenpau","last":"Brodersen","locid":"0f06ffbd-c5c4-4560-aac7-f0bfdf8e86e7","name":"Brodersen, John","orcid":"0000-0001-9369-3376","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Janus Laust","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"25fbeab7-fe04-4aed-80b3-453feba1e361","name":"Thomsen, Janus Laust","orcid":"0000-0002-0745-6815","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bach","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"929fc31a-f5b6-45b8-b3ed-29bd2c4b4bf7","name":"Jensen, Martin Bach","orcid":"0000-0003-2162-7390","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Annette Sofie","key":"davidsenpau","last":"Davidsen","locid":"912ed585-921f-465e-83d4-a06e52697aed","name":"Davidsen, Annette Sofie","orcid":"0000-0002-5434-0997","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35166178"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/02813432.2022.2036483","issn":["02813432","15027724"],"issn_org":["02813432"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Primary Health Care","pages":"67-77","publisher":"Informa Healthcare","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Primary Health Care","volume":"40"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85125228402"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-f7cb8ea6-1e3f-4684-a52d-7161037c7fa5","ku-a6bd4757-6a08-490d-8745-8f8843f061af"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Learning strategies of general practitioners striving to achieve point-of-care ultrasound competence : a qualitative study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-098.43-011837","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: Inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD), encompassing Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis, are chronic, inflammatory diseases of the gastrointestinal tract. We have initiated a Danish population-based inception cohort study aiming to investigate the underlying mechanisms for the heterogeneous course of IBD, including need for, and response to, treatment. METHODS AND ANALYSIS: IBD Prognosis Study is a prospective, population-based inception cohort study of unselected, newly diagnosed adult, adolescent and paediatric patients with IBD within the uptake area of Hvidovre University Hospital and Herlev University Hospital, Denmark, which covers approximately 1 050 000 inhabitants (~20% of the Danish population). The diagnosis of IBD will be according to the Porto diagnostic criteria in paediatric and adolescent patients or the Copenhagen diagnostic criteria in adult patients. All patients will be followed prospectively with regular clinical examinations including ileocolonoscopies, MRI of the small intestine, validated patient-reported measures and objective examinations with intestinal ultrasound. In addition, intestinal biopsies from ileocolonoscopies, stool, rectal swabs, saliva samples, swabs of the oral cavity and blood samples will be collected systematically for the analysis of biomarkers, microbiome and genetic profiles. Environmental factors and quality of life will be assessed using questionnaires and, when available, automatic registration of purchase data. The occurrence and course of extraintestinal manifestations will be evaluated by rheumatologists, dermatologists and dentists, and assessed by MR cholangiopancreatography, MR of the spine and sacroiliac joints, ultrasonography of peripheral joints and entheses, clinical oral examination, as well as panoramic radiograph of the jaws. Fibroscans and dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry scans will be performed to monitor occurrence and course of chronic liver diseases, osteopenia and osteoporosis. ETHICS AND DISSEMINATION: This study has been approved by Ethics Committee of the Capital Region of Denmark (approval number: H-20065831). Study results will be disseminated through publication in international scientific journals and presentation at (inter)national conferences.","author_count":33,"contributors":["Attauabi, Mohamed","Madsen, Gorm Roager","Bendtsen, Flemming","Wewer, Vibeke","Wilkens, Rune","Ilvemark, Johan","Vladimirova, Nora","Jensen, Annette Bøjer","Jensen, Frank Krieger","Hansen, Sanja Bay","Siebner, Hartwig Roman","Nielsen, Yousef Jesper Wirenfeldt","Møller, Jakob M.","Thomsen, Henrik S","Thomsen, Simon Francis","Ingels, Helene Andrea Sinclair","Theede, Klaus","Boysen, Trine","Bjerrum, Jacob Tveiten","Jakobsen, Christian","Dorn-Rasmussen, Maria","Jansson, Sabine","Yao, Yiqiu","Burian, Ewa A.","Møller, Frederik Trier","Fana, Viktoria","Wiell, Charlotte","Terslev, Lene","Østergaard, Mikkel","Bertl, Kristina","Stavropoulos, Andreas","Seidelin, Jakob Benedict","Burisch, Johan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-540719"],"filters":{"added":"2022-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Malmö University"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Child","Cohort Studies","Colitis Ulcerative therapy","Humans","Inflammatory Bowel Diseases diagnosis","Microbiota","Prognosis","Prospective Studies","Quality of Life"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"influence of genetics immunity and the microbiome on the prognosis of inflammatory bowel disease ibd prognosis study the protocol for a copenhagen ibd inception cohort study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"