{"abstract":"The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is formed by brain capillary endothelial cells (BECs) supported by pericytes and astrocytes. The BBB maintains homeostasis and protects the brain against toxic substances circulating in the blood, meaning that only a few drugs can pass the BBB. Thus, for drug screening, understanding cell interactions, and pathology, in vitro BBB models have been developed using BECs from various animal sources. When comparing models of different species, differences exist especially in regards to the transendothelial electrical resistance (TEER). Thus, we compared primary mice, rat, and porcine BECs (mBECs, rBECs, and pBECs) cultured in mono- and co-culture with astrocytes, to identify species-dependent differences that could explain the variations in TEER and aid to the selection of models for future BBB studies. The BBB models based on primary mBECs, rBECs, and pBECs were evaluated and compared in regards to major BBB characteristics. The barrier integrity was evaluated by the expression of tight junction proteins and measurements of TEER and apparent permeability (Papp). Additionally, the cell size, the functionality of the P-glycoprotein (P-gp) efflux transporter, and the expression of the transferrin receptor were evaluated and compared. Expression and organization of tight junction proteins were in all three species influenced by co-culturing, supporting the findings, that TEER increases after co-culturing with astrocytes. All models had functional polarised P-gp efflux transporters and expressed the transferrin receptor. The most interesting discovery was that even though the pBECs had higher TEER than rBECs and mBECs, the Papp did not show the same variation between species, which could be explained by a significantly larger cell size of pBECs. In conclusion, our results imply that the choice of species for a given BBB study should be defined from its purpose, instead of aiming to reach the highest TEER, as the models studied here revealed similar BBB properties.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Thomsen, Maj Schneider","Humle, Nanna","Hede, Eva","Moos, Torben","Burkhart, Annette","Thomsen, Louiza Bohn"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-101849"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the blood brain barrier studied in vitro across species","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0236770","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-101849","identifier":[{"id":"85102619981","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33711041","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-b267742a-fbf2-4eea-8da1-52047049685d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-14B73E9ADB1072A6F63F44174FCB2EA1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0236770","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/406971312/journal.pone.0236770.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology, Neurobiology Research and Drug Delivery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maj Schneider","key":"thomsenmajschneiderpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"500ff43a-765e-4d61-9bbd-f228196ed49d","name":"Thomsen, Maj Schneider","orcid":"0000-0002-8385-7039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nanna","key":"humlenannapau","last":"Humle","locid":"2a97d503-6e2f-4948-879c-e94ae5d7289e","name":"Humle, Nanna","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"hedeevapau","last":"Hede","locid":"31fc1c6e-3bbd-4494-9b40-f66d601bed36","name":"Hede, Eva","orcid":"0000-0002-6072-4970","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Torben","key":"moostorbenpau","last":"Moos","locid":"e51cbd29-97d2-42d8-bbca-11f026378a2f","name":"Moos, Torben","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Annette","key":"burkhartannettepau","last":"Burkhart","locid":"7b6bd909-a011-463c-9e09-3623fee043b3","name":"Burkhart, Annette","orcid":"0000-0003-2086-4693","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louiza Bohn","key":"thomsenlouizabohnpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"c4698d51-553a-4391-90b0-5379982b935f","name":"Thomsen, Louiza Bohn","orcid":"0000-0002-3144-7150","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33711041"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0236770","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"3","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0236770","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85102619981"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-b267742a-fbf2-4eea-8da1-52047049685d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"The blood-brain barrier studied in vitro across species","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-101849","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This study aimed to explore key municipal employees’ experiences and perceptions of how to support socially marginalised men’s healthcare needs. The paper is based on 21 interviews with employees and managers from different policy sectors in a large Danish municipality who either work purposefully with targeting services to men in general or offering services that socially marginalised men will encounter. The findings reveal that regardless of policy sector, the participants perceived that interdisciplinary cooperation across policy sectors was crucial because the socially marginalised men had intertwined social and health challenges. The German sociologist Niklas Luhmann’s theoretical concepts of differentiation and independency was used in the discussion to offer insight into the way systems work and how society handles its own problems. Organisational structures such as functional differentiation with specialization, division of labour, and increased autonomy seem to challenge the cooperation across policy sectors. Personal interorganisational conditions such as personal knowledge emerging from previous collaborative relationships or personal relations, and not the policy sectors’ integrated work programs seemed crucial. Currently affected the flexibility in services that supported socially marginalised men’s healthcare needs. The findings from this study contribute to the current literature on organisational organisation by providing an important opportunity to advance the understanding of healthcare employees within the municipality, particularly regarding how healthcare is organised across varying professional practices within different policy sectors in relation to supporting socially marginalised men’s healthcare needs.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Pedersen, Annette","Vardinghus-Nielsen, Henrik","Curtis, Tine","Grønkjær, Mette","Haslund-Thomsen, Helle Lønstrup"],"cuid":["S02-095.80-004326"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Functional differentiation","Interdisciplinarity","Management","Municipality","Organisationsteori","Outreach","Social ulighed","Sundhedsforskning","socially endangered","socially marginalised men"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"OT":["Aalborg Municipality"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCN"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-UC-UCN"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"healthcare services to socially marginalized men a qualitative study on municipal employees experiences with supporting unmet healthcare needs","src":["aau","ucn"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-UC-UCN"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www-idunn-no.auh.aub.aau.dk/nsf/2021/02/healthcare_services_to_socially_marginalized_men","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-095.80-004326","ids":["aau-58b4baf7-bfb0-4a90-9119-d4bca3739392","ucn-406cf9b6-8b73-4e16-af0d-2e0c4e17a403"],"keyword":["Organisationsteori","Social ulighed","Sundhedsforskning","functional differentiation","interdisciplinarity","management","municipality","outreach","socially endangered","socially marginalised men"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-B61930116386D8AE61CABED442D6366E"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ucn university col