{"abstract":"This article draws on ethnographic material from a study among young Danish adults who have complex psychosocial problems and are not engaged in education or employment. Engaging the concepts of recognition (Honneth 2003, 2006) and care (Tronto 1994), we examine the young adults’ experiences of attitudinal, practical and relational aspects of encounters with professionals and services in the social and health care systems. We track how the young adults are socially visible or invisible as individuals depending on whether professionals show a genuine interest in the young adults’ specific stories and help meeting their needs over time. Social visibility is a prerequisite of recognition (Honneth 2003). We argue that at stake in the young adults’ encounters with professionals and services is the recognition of them as equal members of society, who deserve help with overcoming their problems and have something to contribute with. We examine the consequences of lack of recognition, and we conclude that paradoxically the efforts made by the system to help the young adults in some cases contribute to their experience of marginalization.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Andersen, Julie Høgsgaard","Tjørnhøj-Thomsen, Tine","Reventlow, Susanne","Davidsen, Annette Sofie"],"cuid":["S02-094.29-042811"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Julie Høgsgaard"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"being heard and remembered young adults experiences of recognition in the social and health care systems in denmark","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/320754352/Being_Heard_and_Remembered.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We are grateful for the young adults’ participation in our research. All their names have been changed to ensure their anonymity. The Danish Ethics Committees do not evaluate qualitative research, but this research has been approved by the Danish Data Protection Agency (Journal‐nr.: SUND‐2016‐49). The research was funded by: Jascha Fonden; Region Sjællands Sundhedsvidenskabelige Forskningsfonds Ordinære Pulje; Produktion, Forskning og Innovation, Region Sjælland; Aase and Ejnar Danielsens Fond; Multipraksisudvalget; Fonden for Almen Praksis; Helsefonden; Forskningsenheden for Almen Praksis."],"id":"S02-094.29-042811","identifier":[{"id":"85104821848","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-5eb19a36-ea27-4123-a4b4-2fe2b84fd733","sdu-168c1f37-3e21-4ed6-bce7-449327135a25"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-6314A93A2B1015EC26C2F0F08DFFFCE8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/182446663/etho.12291.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/320754352/Being_Heard_and_Remembered.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, External Centres, Forskningsenheden for Almen Praksis"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Human Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health, Department of Health and Social Context"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Julie Høgsgaard","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"5027e5a4-ff0d-4aba-bec9-06d27c79e0e9","name":"Andersen, Julie Høgsgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-5685-5399","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Tine","key":"tjørnhøjthomsenpau","last":"Tjørnhøj-Thomsen","locid":"bc043d50-4a2b-41c1-a085-da4a674866f3","name":"Tjørnhøj-Thomsen, Tine","orcid":"0000-0003-3621-6682","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Susanne","key":"reventlowpau","last":"Reventlow","locid":"8b7d6185-d9ad-4f0e-9b8f-cd49a30374ef","name":"Reventlow, Susanne","orcid":"0000-0002-4990-142X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Annette Sofie","key":"davidsenpau","last":"Davidsen","locid":"912ed585-921f-465e-83d4-a06e52697aed","name":"Davidsen, Annette Sofie","orcid":"0000-0002-5434-0997","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/etho.12291","issn":["00912131","15481352"],"issn_org":["00912131"],"issue":"4","journal":"Ethos","pages":"459-476","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Ethos","volume":"48"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85104821848"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5eb19a36-ea27-4123-a4b4-2fe2b84fd733","sdu-168c1f37-3e21-4ed6-bce7-449327135a25"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Being Heard and Remembered: Young Adults' Experiences of Recognition in the Social and Health Care Systems in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-094.29-042811","year":2021,"yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: Treatment for estrogen receptor positive (ER+), human epidermal receptor 2 negative (HER2−) metastatic breast cancer (MBC) has improved with the approval of CDK 4/6 inhibitors. Clinical trials with the CDK4/6 inhibitor ribociclib, suggest that 35% to 57.5% of the patients require a dose reduction during ribociclib treatment. Data on the possible consequences of dose reduction concerning efficacy is needed. Methods: A retrospective cohort study on patients with ER+ HER2− MBC from three Danish oncology departments. Data on tolerability and progression-free survival were collected from electronic health records. Results: One hundred and twenty-eight patients with ER+ HER2− MBC who initiated ribociclib treatment between 1st of January 2018 to 31st of March 2020 were included in our analysis. Of these patients, 48.4% required one or more dose reductions. Overall median PFS was 19.2 months (CI-95% 14.3-NR). Patients with one or more dose reductions did not have decreased median PFS (19.2 months, CI-95% 14.3-NR compared to 12.2 months, CI-95% 7.3-NR, p = 0.078). Frequency of adverse events were as previously reported, with grade III and IV neutropenia occurring in 45.3% and 7% of patients, respectively. Patients treated with fulvestrant versus an aromatase inhibitor and patients with lymph node involvement at baseline had lower odds of requiring a dose reduction (ORa = 0.30, CI-95% 0.12–0.73 & ORa = 0.41, CI-95% 0.18–0.89, respectively). Conclusion: Our results indicate that dose reduction of ribociclib is safe and do not compromise the efficacy of the treatment. Furthermore, the study supports translation of results from the MONALEESA trials to patients treated in real-world clinical settings.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Kristensen, Kristoffer B.","Thomsen, Ida Marie Nedergaard","Berg, Tobias","Kodahl, Annette R.","Jensen, Anders Bonde"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-012685"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 cont